          "content": "20"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.163,
            0.139,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.258,
            0.151,
            0.768,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "sum of zeros  = \\alpha + \\beta = - \\frac{b}{a} = \\frac{- ( coefficient of   x)}{ Coefficient of   x^{2}},",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.214,
            0.707,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": "product of zeroes $\\alpha \\beta = \\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{ constant term }{ coefficient of   x^{2}}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.268,
            0.461,
            0.287
          ],
          "content": "Let us consider some examples."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.301,
            0.901,
            0.338
          ],
          "content": "Example 2 : Find the zeroes of the quadratic polynomial $x ^ { 2 } + 7 x + 1 0$ , and verify the relationship between the zeroes and the coefficients."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.345,
            0.298,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": "Solution : We have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.358,
            0.371,
            0.639,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "x^{2}+7x+10=(x+2)(x+5)",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.395,
            0.901,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "$\\mathrm { S o } ,$ the value of $x ^ { 2 } + 7 x + 1 0$ is zero when $x + 2 = 0   or   x + 5 = 0.$ , i.e., when $x = - 2$ or $x = - 5$ . Therefore, the zeroes of $x^{2}+7x+10\\mathrm{are}=2$ and – 5. Now,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.448,
            0.838,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "sum of zeros  = -2 + (-5) = -7 = \\frac{-(7)}{1} = \\frac{( Coefficient of   x)}{ Coefficient of   x^2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.511,
            0.788,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": "product of zeros  = (-2) \\times (-5) = 10 = \\frac{10}{1} = \\frac{ constant term }{ coefficient of   x^2}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.572,
            0.901,
            0.611
          ],
          "content": "Example 3 : Find the zeroes of the polynomial $x ^ { 2 } - 3$ and verify the relationship between the zeroes and the coefficients."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.616,
            0.842,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": "Solution : Recall the identity $a^{2}-b^{2}=(a-b)(a+b)$ . Using it, we can write:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.287,
            0.648,
            0.558,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "x^{2}-3=\\left(x-\\sqrt{3}\\right)\\left(x+\\sqrt{3}\\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.693,
            0.647,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": "$\\mathrm { S o } ,$ the value of $x ^ { 2 } - 3$ is zero when $x = \\sqrt{3}  or  x = -\\sqrt{3}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.731,
            0.576,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the zeroes of $x ^ { 2 } - 3$ are $\\sqrt { 3 }$ and $- { \\sqrt { 3 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.764,
            0.156,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": "Now,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.798,
            0.729,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": "sum of zeros  = \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{3} = 0 = \\frac{-( coefficient of   x)}{ C coefficient of   x^2},",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.861,
            0.8,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "{ \\mathrm { p r o d u c t ~ o f ~ z e r o e s } } = { \\mathrm { ~ \\left( { \\sqrt { 3 } } \\right) \\left( - { \\sqrt { 3 } } \\right) ~ } } = - 3 = { \\frac { - 3 } { 1 } } = { \\frac { { \\mathrm { C o n s t a n t ~ t e r m } } } { { \\mathrm { C o e f f i c i e n t ~ o f ~ } } x ^ { 2 } } } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_10_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
